hadiths[46]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1465","On the authority of Al-Nu’man bin Bashir, may Allah be pleased with them both, on the authority of the Prophet (ﷺ) who said: “Supplication is worship.”"
"1466","On the authority of Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) preferred the comprehensive all-encompassing supplications, and abandoned anything else. [Abu Dawud]."
"1467","On the authority of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Most of the supplications of the Prophet (ﷺ) were: “O Allah, give us good in this world, and good in the hereafter, and protect us from the torment of the Fire” [Al-Bukhari and Muslim]. Muslim added in his narration that he said: If Anas wanted to supplicate with a supplication, he would supplicate with it, and if he wanted to supplicate with a supplication, he would supplicate with it in it."
"1468","On the authority of Ibn Masoud, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to say: “O Allah, I ask You for guidance, piety, chastity, and wealth” [Muslim]."
"1469","On the authority of Tariq bin Ashim, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When a man converted to Islam, the Prophet (ﷺ) taught him prayer, and then commanded him to supplicate with these words: “O Allah, forgive me, have mercy on me, guide me, heal me, and provide for me.” [Muslim]. And in his narration on the authority of Tariq, he heard the Prophet (ﷺ) and a man came to him, He said: O Messenger of Allah, how do I say when I ask my Lord? He said: “Say: Oh Allah, forgive me, have mercy on me, protect me, and provide for me, for these will bring together your world and your afterlife.”"
"1470","The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) supplicated: \Allahumma musarrifal-qulubi"","" sarrif qulubana 'ala ta'atika (O Allah! Controller of the hearts"","" direct our hearts to Your obedience).\"" [Muslim] ."""""
"1471","On the authority of Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, on the authority of the Prophet (ﷺ) he said: “Seek refuge in Allah from the stress of affliction, the attainment of misery, bad decree, and the gloating of enemies” [Al-Bukhari and Muslim]. And in a narration: Sufyan said: I doubt that I added one of them."
"1472","On his authority, he said: the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to say: “O Allah, fix for me my religion, which is the protection of my affairs, and fix for me my world, which contains my livelihood, and fix for me my Hereafter, which is my place of return [to You], and make life an increase for me in all good, and make death a relief for me from all evil” [Muslim]."
"1473","On the authority of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to me: “Say: O Allah, guide me and keep me steadfast [on the straight path].” And in a narration: “O Allah, I ask You for guidance and guidance” [Muslim]."
"1474","On the authority of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to say: “O Allah, I seek refuge in You from inability, laziness, cowardice, senility, and stinginess, and I seek refuge in You from the torment of the grave, and I seek refuge in You from the trials of life and death.” And in a narration: “And the weakness of religion and the dominance of men” ([Muslim].."
"1475","And on the authority of Abu Bakr Al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): Teach me a supplication to pray with in my prayers. He said: “Say: Oh Allah, I have wronged myself greatly, and no one can forgive sins except You, so grant me forgiveness from You, and have mercy on me, for You are the Forgiving, the Merciful” [Al-Bukhari and Muslim]. And in a narration: “And in my house” and it was narrated: “a great deal of injustice” and it was narrated “a great deal” with the triple tha’ and the unified ba’, so he should combine them, so he said: a great deal."
"1476","The Prophet (ﷺ) used to supplicate: \Allahumm-aghfir li khati'ati"","" wajahli"","" wa israfi fi amri"","" wa ma Anta a'lamu bihi minni. Allahumm-aghfir li jiddi wa hazli"","" wa khata'i wa 'amdi"","" wa kullu dhalika 'indi. Allahumm-aghfir li ma qaddamtu wa ma akhkhartu"","" wa ma asrartu"","" wa ma a'lantu"","" wa ma Anta a'lamu bihi minni. Antal-Muqaddimu"","" wa Antal-Mu'akhkhiru; wa Anta 'ala kulli shai'in Qadir (O Allah! Forgive my errors"","" ignorance and immoderation in my affairs. You are better aware of my faults than myself. O Allah! Forgive my faults which I committed in seriousness or in fun deliberately or inadvertently"","" and all of that is with me. O Allah! Grant me pardon for those sins which I committed in the past and I may commit in future"","" which I committed in privacy or in public and all those sins of which You are better aware than me. You are the First"","" You are the Last"","" and You are over all things Omnipotent).\"" [Al-Bukhari and Muslim] ."""""
"1477","The Prophet (ﷺ) used to supplicate (in these words): \Allahumma inni audhu bika min sharri ma 'amiltu"","" wa min sharri ma lam a'mal (O Allah! I seek refuge in You from the evil of that which I have done and the evil of that which I have not done).\"" [Muslim] ."""""
"1478","On the authority of Ibn Omar, may Allah be pleased with him and his father, he said: One of the supplications of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was: “O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the disappearance of Your grace, the change of Your well-being, the suddenness of Your vengeance, and all of Your anger” [Muslim]."
"1479","The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would supplicate: \Allahumma inni a'udhu bika minal-'ajzi wal-kasali"","" wal-bukhli wal-harami"","" wa 'adhabil-qabri. Allahumma ati nafsi taqwaha"","" wa zakkiha Anta khairu man zakkaha"","" Anta waliyyuha wa maulaha. Allahumma inni a'udhu bika min 'ilmin la yanfau'"","" wa min qalbin la yakhsha'u"","" wa min nafsin la tashba'u"","" wa min da'watin la yustajabu laha' [O Allah! I seek refuge in You from the inability (to do good)"","" indolence"","" miserliness"","" decrepitude and torment of the grave. O Allah! Grant me the sense of piety and purify my soul as You are the Best to purify it. You are its Guardian and its Protecting Friend. O Allah! I seek refuge in You from the knowledge which is not beneficial"","" and from a heart which does not fear (You)"","" and from a self/soul that is never satisfied [i.e."","" greed]"","" and from prayer which is not answered] .\"" [Muslim] ."""""
"1480","The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to supplicate: \Allahumma laka aslamtu"","" wa bika amantu"","" wa 'alaika tawakkaltu"","" wa ilaika anabtu"","" wa bika khasamtu"","" wa ilaika hakamtu. Faghfir li ma qaddamtu"","" wa ma akh-khartu"","" wa ma asrartu wa ma a'lantu"","" Antal-Muqaddimu"","" wa Antal-Mu'akhkhiru"","" la ilaha illa Anta (O Allah! to You I submit"","" in You I affirm my faith"","" in You I repose my trust"","" to You I turn in repentance and with Your Help I contend my adversaries and from You I seek judgement. O Allah! Grant me forgiveness for the faults which I made in past and those ones I may commit in the future"","" those which I committed secretly or openly. You are the First"","" You are the Last"","" and there is none worthy of worship except You).\"" Another narration adds: \""La hawla wa la quwwata illa billah (There is no strength to resist evil and no power to do good except through Allah).\"" [Al-Bukhari and Muslim] ."""""
"1481","On the authority of Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to pray with these words: “O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the temptation of Hell, the torment of Hell, and from the evil of wealth and poverty.” [Abu Dawud]"
"1482","On the authority of Ziyad bin Ulaqah, on the authority of his uncle, Qutbah bin Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (ﷺ) used to say: “O Allah, I seek refuge in You from reprehensible morals, deeds, and desires.” Al-Tirmidhi narrated it and said it was a good hadith."
"1483","And on the authority of Shakl bin Humaid, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I said, O Messenger of Allah: Teach me a supplication. He said: “Say: Oh Allah, I seek refuge in You from the evil of my hearing, from the evil of my sight, from the evil of my tongue, from the evil of my heart, and from the evil of me.” [Abu Dawud]"
"1484","On the authority of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to say: “O Allah, I seek refuge in You from leprosy, madness, leprosy, and other bad diseases.” [Abu Dawud]"
"1485","The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to supplicate: \Allahumma inni a'udhu bika minal- ju'i"","" fa-innahu bi'sad-daji'u; wa a'udhu bika minal-khiyanati"","" fa- innaha bi'satil-bitanah' [O Allah! I seek refuge in You from hunger; surely"","" it is the worst companion. And I seek refuge in You from treachery; surely"","" it is a bad inner trait] .\"" [Abu Dawud] ."""""
"1486","And on the authority of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that a slave seeking emancipation (mukatib) came to him and said: I am unable to fulfill my contract of emancipation. So help me. He said: Shall I not teach you words that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) taught me, if you had a debt like a mountain that Allah paid on your behalf? Say: “O Allah, protect me from what is forbidden with your lawfulness, and enrich me with your grace above all else.” [Al-Tirmidhi] He said: It is a good hadith"
"1487","On the authority of Imran bin Al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (ﷺ) taught his father Husain two words with which to pray: “O Allah, inspire me with guidance, and protect me from the evil of my soul” [Al-Tirmidhi]."
"1488","I asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) to teach me a supplication. He (ﷺ) said, \Beg Allah for safety (from all evils in this world and in the Hereafter).\"" I waited for some days and then I went to him again and asked him: \""O Messenger of Allah Teach me to supplicate something from Allah.\"" He said to me"","" \""O Al-`Abbas"","" the uncle of Messenger of Allah! Beseech Allah to give you safety (Al-`afiyah ) in this life and in the Hereafter.\"" [At- Tirmidhi] ."""""
"1489","On the authority of Shahr bin Hawshab, he said: I said to Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, O Mother of the Believers, what was the most frequent supplication of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when he was with you? She said: His most frequent supplication was: “O converter of hearts, make my heart steadfast in your religion” [Al-Tirmidhi]."
"1490","The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \One of Prophet Dawud's supplications was: 'Allahumma inni as'aluka hubbaka"","" wa hubba man yuhibbuka"","" wal-'amalalladhi yuballighuni hubbaka. Allahumm-aj'al hubbaka ahabba ilayya min nafsi"","" wa ahli"","" wa minal-ma'il-baridi (O Allah! I ask You for Your Love"","" the love of those who love You"","" and deeds which will cause me to attain Your Love. O Allah! Make Your Love dearer to me than myself"","" my family and the cold water).\""' [At- Tirmidhi] ."""""
"1491","The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \Recite frequently: 'Ya Dhal-Jalali wal-Ikram! (O You"","" Possessor of glory and honour).\""' [At- Tirmidhi] ."""""
"1492","On the authority of Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed many supplications from which we did not memorize anything. We said: “O Messenger of Allah, you prayed with many supplications from which we did not memorize anything,” and he said: “Shall I not inform you of what brings it all together?” You say: “O Allah, I ask you of the best of which your Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ) asked you, and I seek refuge in You from the evil of which your Prophet sought refuge from.” You are the One who is sought for help, and it is upon you to convey the message, and there is no power or strength except with Allah.” [Al-Tirmidhi]"
"1493","On the authority of Ibn Masoud, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Among the supplications of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was: “O Allah, I ask you for the reasons for your mercy, the assurances of your forgiveness, safety from every sin, the spoils from every righteousness, winning Paradise, and salvation from Hell.” [Narrated by Al-Hakim Abu Abdullah, and he said: an authentic hadith according to the conditions of Muslim]."
"1494","On the authority of Abu Al-Darda’, may Allah be pleased with him, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: “There is no Muslim servant who supplicates for his brother behind the scenes without the angel saying, ‘And you have the same’” [Muslim]."
"1495","On his authority, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to say: “A Muslim’s supplication for his brother in his absence will be answered. At his head is an entrusted angel. Whenever he prays for good for his brother, the entrusted angel says: Amen, and you have the same” [Muslim]."
"1496","On the authority of Osama bin Zaid, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Whenever a favor is done to one of you, and he says to the doer: May Allah reward you with good, then he has conveyed utmost praise.” Al-Tirmidhi narrated it and said it is a good and authentic hadith."
"1497","On the authority of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Do not invoke evil upon yourselves, and do not invoke evil upon your children, and do not invoke evil upon your wealth. Do not agree to an hour from Allah in which He asks for a gift and He responds to you” [Muslim]."
"1498","On the authority of Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “The closest a servant is to his Lord is when he is prostrating, so supplicate often” [Muslim]."
"1499","On his authority, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “One of you will be answered as long as he is not in a hurry. He says, ‘I have called upon my Lord, but He did not answer me.’” [Al-Bukhari and Muslim]. And in a narration by a Muslim, a servant will still be answered as long as he does not pray for sin or severing family ties, as long as he is not in a hurry. It was said, O Messenger of Allah, what is haste? He said, “I have prayed and I have prayed,” but he did not. I see someone who responds to me, then he regrets it and abandons his supplication"
"1500","On the authority of Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: It was said to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): Which supplication should I hear? He said: “The latter part of the night and after the prescribed prayers.” Al-Tirmidhi narrated it and said it was a good hadith."
"1501","On the authority of Ubadah bin Al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “There is no Muslim on earth who supplicates to Allah Almighty with a supplication but that Allah grants it to him, or averts from him an evil similar to it. Unless he supplicates for sin or severing ties of kinship.” Then a man from the people said: “Then we will multiply.” He said: “Allah is more.” Al-Tirmidhi narrated it and said it was a good, authentic hadith."
"1502","On the authority of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to say when distressed: “There is no god but Allah, the Great, the Forbearing, there is no god but Allah, Lord of the Great Throne, there is no god but Allah, Lord of the heavens, Lord of the earth, and Lord of the Noble Throne” [Al-Bukhari and Muslim]."
"1503","On the authority of Abu Muhammad Abd al-Rahman bin Abi Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Companions of the Suffah were poor people, and that the Prophet (ﷺ) once said: “Whoever has food for two, let him go with a third, and whoever has food for four, let him take a fifth with a sixth.” Or as he said: And that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, brought three, and the Prophet (ﷺ) set out with ten, and that Abu Bakr dined at The Prophet (ﷺ) then stayed until he prayed the evening prayer, then returned, and came after the night had passed, Allah willing. His wife said to him: What kept you away from your guests? He said: Did you not feed them? She said: They refused until you came and they were presented to them. He said: So I went and hid, and he said: O Gunther, and he became angry and cursed, and said: Eat, may it not be blessed. By Allah, I will never feed him again. He said: By Allah, we did not take a single morsel except that more increased from below it than (the morsel taken), until they were full, and it became more than it was before. Then Abu Bakr looked at her and said to his wife: O sister of Banu Firas, what is this? She said: No, the apple of my eye is now three times more than it was before! So Abu Bakr ate from it and said: This was only from Satan, meaning his right hand. Then he ate from it. A morsel, then he carried it to the Prophet (ﷺ) and it remained with him in the morning, and there was a covenant between us and my people, so the term passed, so we separated twelve men, with each man having people, Allah knows how many each man had, so they all ate from it. And in a narration: Abu Bakr swore not to feed him, so the woman swore not to feed him, so the guest - or the guests - swore not to feed him, or to feed him until he fed him, so Abu Bakr said Bakr: This is from Satan! So he called for food, and they ate and they ate, and they began not to lift a morsel except that more increased from below it, and he said: O sister of Banu Firas, what is this? She said: The apple of my eye is now more than it is before we eat, so they ate, and he sent it to the Prophet (ﷺ) and he mentioned that he ate some of it. And in a narration: Abu Bakr said to Abd al-Rahman: Attend to your guests, for I am going to the Prophet (ﷺ) so finish serving them their food before I come back. So Abd al-Rahman set off and brought them what he had, and he said: Feed. They said: Where is the master of our house? He said: Feed. They said: We will not eat until the master of our house comes. He said: Accept your villages from us, for if he comes and you do not feed, we will meet with him. They refused, so I knew that he was angry with me, and when he came, I stepped away from him, and he said: What did you do? So they told him, and he said: O Abdul Rahman, but he remained silent, then he said: O Abdul Rahman, and he remained silent, and he said: O Gunther, I swear to you if you had heard my voice when I came! So I left, and I said: Ask your guests, and they said: He spoke the truth, he brought him to us. He said: You only waited for me, and by Allah I will not feed him. tonight, and the others said: By Allah, we will not feed him until you feed him. He said: Woe to you, why do you not accept your food from us? Bring your food. So he brought it, and he placed his hand and said: In the name of Allah. The first is from Satan. So they ate and ate. [Al-Bukhari and Muslim]."
"1504","On the authority of Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “There were among the nations before you people who were inspired (by Allah), so if there is anyone in my nation, then he is Omar” [Narrated by Al-Bukhari, and narrated by Muslim from the narration of Aisha, and in their narration Ibn Wahb said: “Hadithists” meaning: inspired]."
"1505","The inhabitants of Kufah complained to 'Umar (May Allah be pleased with him) against Sa'd bin Abu Waqqas (May Allah be pleased with him) and 'Umar (May Allah be pleased with him) appointed 'Ammar (May Allah be pleased with him) as Governor of Kufah in his place. Their complaint was that he did not even conduct As-Salat (the prayers) properly. 'Umar (May Allah be pleased with him) sent for Sa'd and said to him: \O Abu Ishaq"","" the people claim that you do not offer the Salat properly.\"" Sa'd replied: \""By Allah! I observe Salat according to the Salat of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and I make no decrease in it. I prolong Qiyam (standing) in the first two Rak'ahs of the Isha prayer and shorten in the last ones.\"" 'Umar (May Allah be pleased with him) said: \""This is what I thought of you"","" O Abu Ishaq!\"" Then he sent with him a man (or some men) to Kufah to investigate the matter about him (from the people of Kufah). The inquiry was conducted in every mosque and all the people in these mosques praised him; but in the mosque of the Banu 'Abs"","" a man"","" with the name of Usamah bin Qatadah and surname Abu Sa'dah"","" stood up and said"","" \""Sa'd bin Abu Waqqas did not participate in Jihad and he did not distribute the spoils equitably and did not judge justly.\"" On this Sa'd said: \""I shall make three supplications in respect of him: O Allah! If this slave of Yours is a liar and seeker of notoriety"","" please prolong his life and lengthen his period of adversity and afflict him with trials.\"" (And so did it happen.) Thereafter"","" when the man was asked about his condition he would say"","" \""I am an old man afflicted with trials and overtaken by the curses of Sa'd.\"" 'Abdul-Malik bin 'Umair (a subnarrator) said: I saw this man with eyebrows hung over his eyes as a result of his old age and he walked aimlessly"","" following young girls and winking at them. [Al-Bukhari and Muslim] ."""""
"1506","On the authority of Urwa bin Al-Zubayr, that Arwa bint Aws brought Saeed bin Zaid bin Amr bin Nufayl, may Allah be pleased with him, before Marwan bin Al-Hakam in a dispute, and she claimed that he had taken something from her land. Saeed said: I was taking something from her land after what I heard from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)!? He said: What did you hear from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He says: “Whoever takes an inch of the land unjustly, it will surround him to seventy lands.” Then Marwan said to him: “I will not ask you for proof after this.” Sa’id said: “O Allah, if she is lying, then blind her and kill her in her land.” He said: “She did not die until her sight was gone, and while she was walking in her land, she fell into a pit and died.” (Agreed upon) (on him)). And in a narration by Muslim on the authority of Muhammad bin Zaid bin Abdullah bin Omar, with the meaning that he saw her blind touching the walls, she says: Saeed’s supplication struck me, and that she passed by a well in the house where she disputed with him, and she fell into it and it was her grave."
"1507","My father called me on the evening before (the battle of) Uhud and said: \I perceive that I shall be among the first from among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) to be martyred"","" and after him you are the dearest to me. I am under the burden of debt. Pay it and treat your sisters well.\"" Next morning he was among the first to be killed"","" so I buried him along with another in the same grave. Thereafter"","" I did not like that I should leave him with another in the grave. So I dug up his corpse after six months and he was in the same condition in which he was on the day when I buried him"","" except that there was a scratch on his ear. Then I buried him in a separate grave. [Al- Bukhari] ."""""
"1508","And on the authority of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that two men from the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) left the presence of the Prophet (ﷺ) on a dark night with something like two lamps in their hands. When they separated, each of them had one (lamp) with him until he came to his family. [Al-Bukhari] From a chain of narrations, and in some of them, the two men are Usayd ibn Hudayr and Abbad ibn Bishr, may Allah be pleased with them."
"1509","On the authority of Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent a group of ten men as a secret detachment, and Asim bin Thabit Al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, commanded them. They set off until they were at Al-Huddah, between Usfan and Mecca, and they were informed of a group of Hudhayl called Banu Lahyan. So they sent out to them about a hundred archers, and they tracked them down. When Asim sensed them, Asim sensed them. And his companions took refuge in a place, and the people surrounded them, and they said: Come down, and surrender yourselves, and we give you our covenant and promise that we will not kill any of you. Then Asim bin Thabit said: O people, as for me, I will not go down under the protection of an infidel: Oh Allah, tell your Prophet about us, so they shot them with arrows and killed Asim, and three people came down to them in accordance with the covenant and the covenant, including Khubayb. And Zaid bin Al-Dathnah and another man. When they were able to control them, they released the strings of their bows and tied them to them. The third man said: This is the beginning of treachery, and by Allah I will not accompany you. I have an example in these people. He wanted the dead. They dragged him and tried to persuade him, but he refused to accompany them, so they killed him, and they set off with Khubayb and Zaid bin Al-Dathnah, until they sold them in Mecca after the battle of Badr, the sons of Al-Harith bin Amir bin Nawfal bin Abd Manaf bought Khubayb, and it was Khubayb who killed Al-Harith on the day of Badr, so Khubayb stayed with them captive until they agreed to kill him, so he borrowed from some of Al-Harith’s daughters a razor to shave with, so she lent it to him, and her young son crawled to him while she was unaware until he came to him, and she found him sitting on his thigh with the razor in his hand, and she was terrified, as Khubayb recognized it, so he said, “Am I afraid that I will kill him?” I would not have done that. She said: By Allah, I have never seen a better captive than Khubayb. By Allah, I found him one day eating a bunch of grapes in his hand, and he was bound with iron, and there was no fruit in Mecca. She used to say: He was a sustenance. Allah had provided him with Khubayb. When they took him out of the Haram to kill him in the enclosure, Khubayb said to them: Let me pray two rak’ahs, so they left him, so he kneeled two rak’ahs, and he said: By Allah, had it not been for Do not think that I am not afraid, I would have increased. O Allah, count them in number, and kill them as they are scattered, and do not leave any of them alive. And he said: I do not care when I am killed as a Muslim  on which side Allah has my death, and that is in the essence of Allah, and if He wills  He will bless the limbs of the dead man, and it was Khubayb who enacted the prayer for every Muslim who was killed patiently, and he - meaning the Prophet (ﷺ) - told his companions on the day they received news of them (the martyrs). They told them, and people from the Quraysh sent to Asim bin Thabit when they heard that he had been killed to bring something known from him, and he had killed a man of their greats, so Allah sent for Asim a canopy like a swarm of bees, and it protected him from their messengers, but they were not able to cut off anything from him. [Al-Bukhari] His saying: Al-Huda: a place, and the canopy means the clouds, and the back means the bees. And his saying: “Kill them scattered” by breaking the b and opening it, and whoever broke it, he said: It is the plural of bada with the kasra of the b, which is the share, and its meaning is: Kill them in divided shares, each one of them has a share, and whoever opened it, he said, it means: dispersed in the killing, one after one, out of dispersion. On the subject there are many authentic hadiths that have been mentioned in their places in this book, including the hadith of the boy who used to come to the monk. And the magician, and among them is the hadith of Jurayj, and the hadith of the people of the cave to whom the rock closed, and the hadith of the man who heard a voice in the clouds saying: Water so-and-so’s garden, and other than that. The evidence in this chapter is many and well-known, and Allah grant us success."
"1510","I never heard 'Umar (May Allah be pleased with him) relating anything that he conceived it to be so-and-so, but that it proved to be as he had conceived it.\ [Al-Bukhari] ."""""
